{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/marker-propagation-boundary-validation.md", "content": "# Marker propagation and boundary validation

In noisy ARC grids with one or more special-color seeds, do not assume the marker spreads through every cell that is not another color. A useful competing mechanism is flood-fill through only the designated background color, with other non-background colors acting as barriers. Simulate both candidate connectivity domains and compare the complete predicted footprint against all demo outputs, including untouched cells and grid-edge contact. Preserve original seeds and all cells outside the proven domain. Only project a propagation rule onto the test after reproducing each demo exactly; check shape, row widths, palette, and changed-cell count mechanically.

A propagation footprint that is surprisingly large is not itself evidence for unrestricted fill. Determine whether the allowed-color component is enclosed or connected to the boundary. If demonstrations fail to distinguish allowed-color connectivity from unrestricted flood fill, spend a paid demo only if those hypotheses generate materially different test outputs and a new example is likely to settle the distinction.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/marker-propagation-boundary-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review-discipline", "file": "references/post-error-same-premise.md", "content": "# Post-error: do not resubmit the same premise

When a submission is marked incorrect, treat it as evidence that the complete proposed transformation is not sufficiently supported. Reconstruct the demos' changed and unchanged cells, then separate what they establish from what they do not establish about the test's more complex structure. Do not immediately repeat the same grid or make a cosmetic adjustment while retaining the failed premise. Reopen materially different rule families, and request another paid demo only if its possible outcomes discriminate among those families and can change the test prediction. A second submission should rely on new evidence or a genuinely distinct hypothesis that has been replayed against all demonstrations.

In the reviewed instance, simple stripe demonstrations showed selective extension/recoloring behavior but did not warrant applying a guessed global pattern to the test markers. The subsequent submission retained the original unsupported prediction despite the explicit post-error reassessment requirement; this is precisely the avoidable failure mode.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review-discipline/references/post-error-same-premise.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review-discipline", "file": "references/demo-evidence-gate-retrospective.md", "content": "# Returned-demo evidence gate: review lesson

In this stream, repeated reference browsing displaced analysis of five paid demonstration pairs, followed by another speculative submission after the first answer was wrong. The demos had substantial variation, but none was converted into a coordinate-level delta ledger before the next action. The reusable correction is to treat demo return as a mandatory checkpoint: extract changed coordinates and informative unchanged candidates; compare with prior examples; update complete test predictions; only then choose request versus submit. If consulting a reference, do it once to resolve a specific question and return immediately to the grids. This documents a process failure and corrective discipline, not a solved transformation for this puzzle.
